hello and welcome to the daily climate  show coming up  a risk of droughts leading bodies decide  against water restrictions but stress  people must use water wisely to protect  supplies  a climate disaster more than 6 000  residents evacuate california as a  wildfire the size of san francisco rips  16 acres of land  and future green living saudi arabia  announces plans for a zero carbon city  [Music]  welcome to the daily climate show where  we track the changes happening to our  world hear from those affected and  report on the race for solutions so with  unusually hot weather and minimal  rainfall comes the serious risk of  drought it's not a common occurrence in  the uk but then it's been an unusual  summer dry conditions and little chance  of rain over the next month have  prompted water companies to start  putting drought plans into action with a  strong push to watch how much water  we're using and there's concerns amongst  farmers that this is only the beginning  it's incredibly dry um we are seeing  non-existent grass growth now so the  livestock farmers around me near me are  having to use um winter rations uh you  know what they what they what they've  harvested recently  what they would feed in the winter  they're starting to dip into now because  they've got no grass growth  um that you know the conditions are  tinderbox dry  our correspondent ashna hurinag joins us  now so ashley how much water are we all  using water is collected in the uk in  lots of different ways through rivers  through reservoirs and in underground  sources too but here in the uk we use an  awful lot of it it's estimated that we  actually use 14 billion tons of water  every single day but we don't always  actually use all of it and when it comes  out of our taps or in industry for that  matter because 3.17 billion liters are  lost every single day due to leaks now  all of that supply is threatened by  these hot and dry conditions that we're  getting which is why we're going to  bring in and tell you about the drought  stages  now at the moment the majority of  england  except the north west is sitting within  what's called a prolonged dry weather  spell so we're seeing things like rivers  and reservoirs being withered and some  restrictions are on the horizon  potentially that's been brought on by a  very early dry spring an early dry  summer too the environment agency today  has called a meeting if you like between  their national drought group to discuss  preventative measures they're looking at  agreeing about agreeing things like  reducing the amount of water that  everyone uses  if that does happen we could see if  there is no rainfall for the next two to  three weeks  the environment agency is saying that we  could start to enter that secondary  drought phase as soon as august okay so  are we close then to a host pipe ban  we are keeping an eye on the uk's water  supply that's not just the government  but also the invite the environment  agency specifically this is a map  showing england's rainfall now what's  key to this is that we have had the  driest period between november and june  since 1976.  it looks like the southern the central  areas  in particular are struggling the most  with this the soil is drier than usual  for this time of the year more than 75  percent of groundwater  those river levels in those groundwater  sites are very low there are some rivers  that are exceptionally low namely the  river north tyne in the river cam or a  froome of avon those are kind of hot  spot areas really and the environment  agency and other government bodies are  really watching those river levels  because it's one of our main sources of  supply  if they do start to bring in talk of  drought we may start to see things like  a limit on the amount of  boats or the way that boats are able to  travel through rivers and canals the  water industry may start restricting  non-essential domestic and commercial  use of water and lots of people are  talking about the drought 1976 will that  happen again yeah the great drought 1936  many people will remember this time i  mean how can you not the lands were  cracked the gardens were withered it was  an exceptionally i guess shocking time  because you're used to seeing droughts  in other parts of the world and to talk  about it in the uk and talk about it  right now in the uk obviously is a huge  concern we know that the ground is  warming up and the air around us is  warming up and to think about the  knock-on effect is no doubt concerning  the truth is we are quite far away from  reaching the scenes that we did in 1976  that's that at that time we had a  significant depletion of groundwater  sites but also the supply of water  and it happened because there was no  rain from the december of that year  and the environment agency and people  like the met office say if there is  rainfall within the next two to three  weeks this isn't something that we  necessarily have to worry about but the  crucial thing is how much rain will fall  and if it falls at all if over the next  few weeks we do start to see some rain  which is looking you know it's not  impossible for it to rain over the  summer then we may not see  any form of drought at all but it is  worth having um in the back of our minds  potentially indeed thank you ashna very  much uh time now for the day's other  climate news now  now despite talk of a potential drought  floods in the uk still pose a serious  threat now the government has pledged  100 million pounds to help protect  properties at risk of flooding the  frequently flooded alliance will be used  to improve smaller communities spread  across a smaller area and require a more  complex flood scheme 2 000 flood  defenses are expected to be constructed  across the country by 2027.  now the government is facing fresh legal  action over consent being given to  develop a large north sea gas field  regulators gave the go-ahead in june for  shell to develop the new jackdaw field  of the charity greenpeace who've  launched a legal challenge described the  approval of the site as a scandal and  they claim the government failed to  consider the impact emissions from  burning the gas would have on the  climate  and more than 300 people have been  killed in pakistan as the country  suffers more than five weeks of monsoon  rains and flash flooding now the wet  season runs from july through to  september has called rivers to swell and  has destroyed motorways and bridges and  rain of course is essential to help  crops grow and replenish dams and other  water reservoirs in pakistan  saudi arabia's crown prince has  announced plans for a zero carbon city  which will have vertical layers of  building stretching over a hundred miles  long if successful saudi arabia claimed  nyon will be the first city to be free  of roads cars and any form of emissions  but critics have accused the saudi  arabia prince that the driving force  behind the arm of green washing and  making grand promises about the  environment which are unrealistic  possible  policies even to curb emissions  now eu energy ministers have agreed to  ration national natural gas this winter  to help protect themselves against  supply cuts by russia as moscow  continues its invasion of ukraine now  they agree to a voluntary gas reduction  of at least 15 percent at less than 24  hours after russia's state controlled  energy firm gasprom announced a steep  reduction in gas supplies from wednesday  it comes as eu leaders accuse russian  president vladimir putin of attempting  to blackmail european countries for  supporting ukraine now australia's new  prime minister  has set out how he intends to tackle  climate change as parliament sat for the  first time since he was elected anthony  albanese announced plans for new  legislation that would force australia  to reduce its greenhouse gas emissions  by 43 below 2005 levels by the end of  the decade however it will be a bumpy  road for the new prime minister the  green party have described the target as  modest and and they don't think the  plans are nearly as ambitious enough but  with the political situation as it  stands the bill would need their support  we have our mandate  which is very clear our mandate is for  43  by 2030 we've said of course the nature  of these targets are that it's a floor  not a ceiling but we actually need to  have a plan  to get change you can't just come up  with a figure i will come up with a sort  of thought bubble  our policy is well thought through  it was announced it was campaigned upon  indeed  it received a mandate  now in california thousands of  firefighters have been battling to  contain fires in what's been the latest  example the devastating effects of  climate change across the world now  yosemite is the main area under threat  from fires on both fronts so the oak  fire started on friday near mid pines  and has spread  to over 21  square miles only 10 is currently  contained and the washburn fire 12 miles  east of yosemite is now around 87  contained after burning for nearly two  weeks now currently there are 2 000  firefighters battling the blaze along  with a large number of emergency  vehicles our correspondent joe pike has  been speaking to residents affected  in mariposa county  even before you see what has happened  here you can smell it the ash blowing  through the air the trees that are still  smoking  it is a stark  sobering sight this trail of devastation  wrought  by this fire a charred car park of quad  bikes and sports cars and pickup trucks  all completely destroyed you imagine  that at one stage this was quite  colorful colorful vehicles green trees  but now it's black and gray it's all  monochrome and if you need a sense of  quite how hot it must have been at the  heart of the fire just look at this  trail of what at some point must have  been a molten metal coming from a  charred bicycle  this part of the fire is now under  control even though we still can see  some flames we can still see smoke of  course firefighters have now moved on to  far bigger blazes which continue to move  through thousands of acres of land  if you talk to people at this evacuation  center they saw the signs an intense  drought parched vegetation low humidity  and high heat this has long been a  punishing environment where wildfires  were possible they were a threat but now  climate change has helped make them  almost inescapable  you as a family  feeling in a very real emotive way the  effects of climate change the effects of  all of our behavior  to people  climate change don't affect them they  say you know if he doesn't affect me it  doesn't matter  you know yeah but it's affecting both of  you but it's affecting it's affecting  everybody affecting everybody but they  don't realize this sometimes and it's  gonna affect our grandkids you know  we're supposed to be looking out for  seven generations and we're not  you know we've been um  activists  for a long time  for uh for the earth and climate change  and  uh the way of our people you know  and against the pipelines all that yeah  you know pipelines cutting of trees and  everything else  but people don't respect that  and just before we go is there anything  you want to know about climate change  what confuses you or mystifies you  scan the qr code on your screen right  now to submit your questions to us and  we'll do our best to answer them or you  can go to any of the sky news social  media accounts where you can add your  questions in the comments so do get in  touch well that's all from us today  thanks for watching we'll see you next  time bye

